WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:07.293
"Bless you for coming," Monsieur Jondrette said as his wife led Monsieur Leblanc into their apartment.

2
00:00:07.294 --> 00:00:16.959
"Yes, bless you," said Madame Jondrette. "You have no idea how much we appreciate your helping the Jondrette family."

3
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:25.451
"Jondrette?" Monsieur Leblanc turned to Monsieur Jondrette in confusion. "I thought you said your name was Fabantou."

4
00:00:25.452 --> 00:00:35.524
"Fabantou, Jondrette, what does it matter? As you may recall, I’m an actor. Jondrette is my stage name. Now please sit down."

5
00:00:35.525 --> 00:00:40.699
Marius watched as Monsieur Leblanc took a seat in one of his own chairs.

6
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:44.937
Marius kept his hand still on the pistol in his waistcoat.

7
00:00:44.938 --> 00:00:53.254
"Now I’m wondering, kind sir," Jondrette went on, "if you’d be interested in this valuable painting . . ."

8
00:00:53.255 --> 00:01:00.175
As Jondrette began to talk, a man quietly entered the room and took a seat on a bed.

9
00:01:00.176 --> 00:01:03.028
Leblanc turned around and stared at him.

10
00:01:03.029 --> 00:01:04.659
"Who’s that?"

11
00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:12.318
"Oh, he’s just one of the neighbors. Now where was I? Oh yes, the painting. Allow me to show it to you."

12
00:01:12.319 --> 00:01:19.167
As Jondrette described the wonders of this work of art, another man slipped quietly into the room.

13
00:01:19.168 --> 00:01:28.302
Again Monsieur Leblanc turned to look at these suspicious characters, and again Monsieur Jondrette reassured him that they were just neighbors.

14
00:01:28.303 --> 00:01:35.223
Monsieur Leblanc rose to his feet. "This is an old inn sign worth only a few francs."

15
00:01:35.224 --> 00:01:40.135
Suddenly the door was thrown open and three more thugs entered the room.

16
00:01:40.136 --> 00:01:48.795
One carried an iron club; the second carried a butcher’s cleaver; and the third held an enormous key stolen from a prison.

17
00:01:48.796 --> 00:01:52.874
"Is everything ready?" asked Monsieur Jondrette.

18
00:01:52.875 --> 00:01:57.278
"Yes, we have the carriages," said the man with the cleaver.

19
00:01:57.279 --> 00:02:02.611
Jondrette suddenly lunged at Leblanc. "Do you recognize me?"

20
00:02:02.612 --> 00:02:07.944
The older man studied Jondrette. "No, I don’t think so."

21
00:02:07.945 --> 00:02:19.613
"My name is not Fabantou or Jondrette. My name is Thenardier, and I was the innkeeper at Montfermeil. Now do you know me?"

22
00:02:19.614 --> 00:02:25.263
Leblanc flushed slightly but stayed calm. "No more than before."

23
00:02:25.264 --> 00:02:32.581
Thenardier’s revelation had little effect on Leblanc, but it completely stunned Marius.

24
00:02:32.582 --> 00:02:39.764
He staggered and almost fell off the table. Here was the man who had saved his father’s life!

25
00:02:39.765 --> 00:02:45.327
Here was the man whom he had been searching for, and this man turned out to be a criminal!

26
00:02:45.328 --> 00:02:52.414
Meanwhile Thenardier was pacing before Leblanc in an insane, frenzied triumph.

27
00:02:52.415 --> 00:03:01.486
"Well, I remember you," he continued. "One Christmas night you came to my inn and took away Fantine’s child, the Lark.

28
00:03:01.487 --> 00:03:07.645
You gave me fifteen hundred francs for that girl, but she was worth much more.

29
00:03:07.646 --> 00:03:12.463
You may dress like a humble man, but I know you’re a millionaire."

30
00:03:12.464 --> 00:03:20.804
"I’m not a millionaire," protested Monsieur Leblanc, "and I wouldn’t have been mixed up with a crook like you."

31
00:03:20.805 --> 00:03:27.638
"A crook?" yelped Thenardier. "I’m a war hero." He thumped the painting.

32
00:03:27.639 --> 00:03:35.130
"This picture was done by a famous artist, and it shows me rescuing General Pontmercy at the Battle of Waterloo."

33
00:03:35.131 --> 00:03:44.019
Marius shuddered. There was no doubt left; this horrible man was indeed the one who had saved his father.

34
00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:49.265
"It still looks like a sign to me," said Monsieur Leblanc.

35
00:03:49.266 --> 00:03:51.649
"Why, you miserable old—"

36
00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:56.257
"Is it time to take care of him now?" interrupted the man with the club.

37
00:03:56.258 --> 00:04:01.844
Before Thenardier could respond, Monsieur Leblanc jumped up and ran to the window.

38
00:04:01.845 --> 00:04:08.519
He opened it and was halfway out, when three men seized him and wrestled him back into the room.

39
00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:12.003
They threw him onto a bed and tied him up.

40
00:04:12.004 --> 00:04:20.424
Marius watched all this in horror. Should he fire the pistol? He wanted to rescue the brave father of the woman he loved.

41
00:04:20.425 --> 00:04:26.662
But if he did that, then the police would arrest Thenardier, who had saved his father’s life.

42
00:04:26.663 --> 00:04:30.075
Thenardier sat down on the bed next to Leblanc.

43
00:04:30.076 --> 00:04:38.329
"I think we can come to an understanding. In a moment we’ll untie one hand so you can write a note."

44
00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:46.559
Leblanc remained silent while Madame Thenardier brought a pen and paper, and her husband dictated the following message:

45
00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.059
My dear daughter, Come immediately.

46
00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:54.226
The person who gives you this note is instructed to bring you to me.

47
00:04:54.227 --> 00:04:59.535
"Now sign it," said Monsieur Thenardier. "What’s your name anyway?"

48
00:04:59.536 --> 00:05:03.309
"Urbain Fabre," replied Monsieur Leblanc.

49
00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.630
Marius stared at the name.

50
00:05:05.631 --> 00:05:14.281
The initials on his precious handkerchief were U.F. Apparently he’d been wrong to assume it belonged to the daughter.

51
00:05:14.282 --> 00:05:20.368
Thenardier gave the note to his wife, who hurried away with the man carrying the cleaver.

52
00:05:20.369 --> 00:05:25.368
"Now let me explain what’s going to happen," said Thenardier.

53
00:05:25.369 --> 00:05:31.201
"My wife will give the note to your daughter, who will accompany her in a carriage.

54
00:05:31.202 --> 00:05:38.201
Somewhere on the edge of the city, the Lark will be transferred to another carriage and taken to an undisclosed location.

55
00:05:38.202 --> 00:05:47.526
My wife will return here, and as soon as you give me two hundred thousand francs, the Lark will be released."

56
00:05:47.527 --> 00:05:52.344
Leblanc, whose real name, of course, was Jean Valjean, said nothing.

57
00:05:52.345 --> 00:06:01.595
"By the way," said Thenardier, "if you have me arrested, my friend will take care of the Lark, if you know what I mean.

58
00:06:01.596 --> 00:06:04.511
Her fate is in your hands."

59
00:06:04.512 --> 00:06:11.923
Marius was in shock. These hoodlums were kidnapping his beloved, but they weren’t bringing her back here?

60
00:06:11.924 --> 00:06:15.154
One of them was going to carry her off and harm her?

61
00:06:15.155 --> 00:06:22.646
He wanted to fire the pistol, but what good would it do? The hideous man with the cleaver would still have the Lark.

62
00:06:22.647 --> 00:06:27.987
Suddenly he heard the door slam downstairs and someone running up the stairs.

63
00:06:27.988 --> 00:06:33.067
Madame Thenardier burst into her apartment, followed by the man with the cleaver.

64
00:06:33.068 --> 00:06:40.877
"False address!" she said breathlessly. "No one at that place had ever heard of Urbain Fabre!"

65
00:06:40.878 --> 00:06:45.805
Marius breathed a sigh of relief because the Lark was safe.

66
00:06:45.806 --> 00:06:52.369
"A false address!" exclaimed Monsieur Thenardier. "What did you hope to gain by that?"

67
00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:59.210
"Time!" said Valjean. At that moment he shook off the ropes, which he had somehow managed to cut.

68
00:06:59.211 --> 00:07:03.961
Fastened by only one leg to the bed, he reached for the chisel in the fire.

69
00:07:03.962 --> 00:07:08.536
He pressed it to his forearm as the others recoiled in horror.

70
00:07:08.537 --> 00:07:13.194
"Do to me what you will," he said. "I’m not afraid."

71
00:07:13.195 --> 00:07:20.011
"It’s time to kill him," muttered Madame Thenardier as her husband reached for the carving knife in the drawer.

72
00:07:20.012 --> 00:07:25.018
Marius looked wildly about him. Was now the moment to fire the pistol?

73
00:07:25.019 --> 00:07:33.162
But what about the Lark? Suddenly he spotted a piece of paper on his table. It said, "The cops are here."

74
00:07:33.163 --> 00:07:37.496
He wrapped it around a piece of plaster and tossed it through the hole in the wall.

75
00:07:37.497 --> 00:07:40.829
"What was that?" cried Madame Thenardier.

76
00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:47.410
"Look!" said her husband. "It’s Eponine’s writing. She must have thrown it through the window to warn us."

77
00:07:47.411 --> 00:07:54.323
The thug with the enormous key tossed it on the floor and signaled the others. "It’s time to get out of here!"

78
00:07:54.324 --> 00:07:59.394
Quickly another thug unrolled the rope ladder and attached it to the window.

79
00:07:59.395 --> 00:08:03.077
"Not so fast!" said a voice at the door.

80
00:08:03.078 --> 00:08:11.736
To Marius’ relief, it belonged to Javert. A squad of policemen rushed into the room and handcuffed the thugs.

81
00:08:11.737 --> 00:08:16.053
"Now where is that man you were holding hostage?" asked Javert.

82
00:08:16.054 --> 00:08:23.229
An officer ran to the window, where the rope ladder was still trembling. "He seems to have escaped, sir."

